// @emit-error call to unknown function
// THE REMAINING HALF OF THE SPLIT: the STORE widths. The checker declares
// seventeen memory intrinsics; the emitter lowers twelve of them —
// `__store_i32__`, `__load_i32__`, `__log__`, the seven new load widths
// (`__load_i8__`/`__load_u8__`/`__load_i16__`/`__load_u16__`/`__load_i64__`/
// `__load_f32__`/`__load_f64__`), `__memory_size__` and `__memory_grow__`.
//
// The four that still pass `vl check` clean and then fail at EMIT are
// `__store_i64__`, `__store_f32__` (this one), `__store_f64__` and
// `__store_string__`, plus `__log_string__`. They are deliberately outside the
// first slice: a program stores i32 words and reads them back at every width,
// which is what makes the load matrix measurable without also committing to a
// store surface. `docs/internals/buffer-design.md` §C2 has the full table and
// §"the smallest first slice" says why the stores wait.
//
// The diagnostic still reads like a typo'd identifier rather than "this builtin
// has no implementation", which remains the half worth fixing first (§B1).
__store_f32__(0, 1.5 as f32)
print(__load_i32__(0))
